From 949c459bab197ca0e2191e93fb1a61ffee5ea5ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Drew T <50529377+Druthulu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 11:24:21 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Add --score-mode reloc-masked: relocation-masked scoring
 for MIPS

The default scorer only treats a MIPS operand as a relocation when the field
contains a "." (field_matches_any_symbol). Projects whose symbols don't have
one (e.g. splat's func_80012345) get a register penalty for every relocation
slot with a different symbol name, so the score can't reach 0 even for a
candidate that would link to the same bytes. In practice the search then
wanders around a floor: on one 225-instruction function the base score was
~225, the best ~210, and it never hit 0 although a byte-exact answer existed.

RelocMaskedScorer (src/reloc_scorer.py) compares the raw instruction words
instead, masking the fields the linker fills in based on the target's
relocation records (R_MIPS_26: opcode only; HI16/LO16/PC16/LITERAL/GPREL16/
GOT16/CALL16: opcode+rs+rt) and requiring the relocation symbol to match at
those positions. A j against .text is compared by its target relative to the
function start. Score = differing positions + length difference, 0 iff the
candidate links to the same bytes.

It's a Scorer subclass, so Permuter/Candidate are unchanged. Selected with
--score-mode reloc-masked or score_mode = "reloc-masked" in settings.toml;
the default is unchanged. Refused together with -J since the remote
evaluator uses the default scorer. MIPS only. --debug prints the differing
instructions.

Tests use an embedded objdump -drz listing of a small gcc 2.7.2 object so no
cross toolchain is needed.
